compat=add('lem-regular-base-dualizing-traces-compose-on-rational-modifications','Dualizing traces compose and become isomorphisms on rational modifications',['lem-projective-regular-local-base-coherent-duality-by-embedding','lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base','lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','lem-local-normal-surface-modification-dimension-and-projective-cohomology','lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading','lem-surface-flat-base-change-coherent-cohomology-by-cech','thm-proper-pushforward-coherent','thm-serre-vanishing'],r'''Let $R$ be regular local of dimension two, $A$ finite normal local over $R$, and let $g:X'\to X$ be a morphism of projective normal modifications over $A$. Their regular-base dualizing complexes are independent of the chosen projective embeddings up to the unique isomorphism preserving their duality pairings. There is a canonical trace $Rg_*D_{X'}\to D_X$, and traces compose. If every closed local ring of $X$ is rational, this trace is an isomorphism, giving $g_*\omega_{X'}=\omega_X$, $R^qg_*\omega_{X'}=0$ for $q>0$, and an adjoint evaluation $g^*\omega_X\to\omega_{X'}$. These evaluations compose along rational modifications.''',r'''For every bounded coherent K on X, the actual regular-base duality pairing gives Hom_X(K,D_X[j])=Hom_R(RΓ_XK,R[2+j]). Thus D_X represents that fixed functor, independently of embedding; Yoneda gives the unique pairing-preserving identification. Similarly D_X′ represents K↦Hom_X(Rg*K,D_X), since RΓ_XRg*K=RΓ_X′K, proper coherence makes Rg*K bounded coherent, and the two evaluation dualities give this equality naturally. The identity of D_X′ therefore corresponds to a trace Rg*D_X′→D_X. Applying these representing equalities successively proves composition of traces: both composites correspond to the identity of the final representing object. This construction agrees with finite closed-immersion coinduction, by its evaluation-at-one adjunction. If X has rational closed local rings, localize g at each point. At codimension-one points it is an isomorphism. At closed points the local proper modification has H0 equal to the normal local ring, H1 zero by rationality, and no higher cohomology by the two-affine projective local surface cover. Flat Čech localization therefore gives Rg*O_X′=O_X. Test the trace on F=O_X(m) and every shift. Hom_X(F,Rg*D_X′) is Hom_X′(g*F,D_X′), by the locally free pullback/direct-image adjunction, and then equals Hom_R(RΓ_X′g*F,R[2]). The finite Čech projection formula and Rg*O=O identify RΓ_X′g*F with RΓ_XF, making this precisely Hom_X(F,D_X); under evaluation it is the induced trace map. Hence trace is an isomorphism on these tests. Its bounded coherent cone is zero: for a nonzero highest cohomology sheaf of that cone, a sufficiently high positive twist has nonzero global sections and all positive cohomology zero by Serre, contradicting the tests by negative twists and shifts. Thus trace is an isomorphism. Concentration D=ω[2] gives the sheaf assertions. Its inverse followed by ordinary pullback/direct-image evaluation defines g*ω_X→ω_X′. Counits compose and their trace identifications are the ones just proved, so these maps compose. This supplies the exact rational-case relative trace needed for canonical principalization without importing an unspecified proper f-shriek theorem.''')
curve=add('lem-cm-projective-curve-canonical-positive-twist-vanishing-generation','Positive canonical twists on projective Cohen–Macaulay curves',['thm-serre-duality-for-coherent-sheaves-on-projective-cm-scheme','lem-projective-pure-cm-dualizing-complex-concentration','lem-surface-flat-base-change-coherent-cohomology-by-cech','lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','cor-cohen-macaulay-modules-have-no-embedded-associated-primes','thm-cohomology-projective-space-twisting-sheaves','thm-nakayama-lemma','def-degree-invertible-sheaf-proper-dimension-one'],r'''Let $E$ be a projective pure CM curve over a field $\kappa$, with $H^0(E,O_E)=\kappa$ and canonical module $\omega_E$. If $L$ is globally generated and nontrivial, $H^1(E,\omega_E\otimes L)=0$. If $L$ is very ample with $\deg_\kappa L\ge2$, then $\omega_E\otimes L$ is globally generated. These statements allow nonreduced $E$.''',r'''Duality identifies H1(ω_E⊗L)∨ with Hom(ω_E⊗L,ω_E)=H0(L^-1), using canonical-module homothety. A nonzero section t of L^-1 and finitely many generators of L have some product st nonzero: otherwise the generators force t=0 locally. Since H0(O)=κ, that product is a nonzero scalar and trivializes L, contradiction. This proves vanishing. For generation, first pass to an algebraic closure. Field base change preserves H0(O)=κ, degree and generation by flat Čech cohomology; the ambient finite free polynomial resolutions identify the base change of ω_E with the canonical module. Pure CM survives field extension: at local rings flat-local depth additivity gives CM, since the zero-dimensional algebraic-extension fibres are CM and dimension is unchanged. Fix a closed geometric point e. Very ampleness gives a hyperplane section s vanishing at e and nonzero at every generic point of E: over the infinite algebraically closed field the hyperplanes through e that contain a given positive-dimensional component form a proper linear subspace; finitely many such subspaces cannot cover the parameter space. CM excludes embedded associated points, so s is regular. Let D=Z(s). Cartier coinduction from its two-term line-bundle resolution gives ω_D=(ω_E⊗L)|D, with finite-module duality at each Artinian stalk. The length of H0(ω_D) equals length of H0(O_D), which is deg L≥2 by the multiplication-by-s sequence and Euler-characteristic degree. Put F=ω_E⊗L. Its sequence 0→ω_E→F→ω_D→0 has quotient of dimension at least two and H1(ω_E) of dimension one. Let F′ be the subsheaf generated by global sections of F and the image of ω_E. Some global section has nonzero image in ω_D since the boundary has target dimension one. If H1(F′)≠0, the map H1(ω_E)→H1(F′) is an isomorphism. Serre duality representing H1(-)∨ supplies F′→ω_E whose composite with ω_E→F′ is the identity (homothety plus its nonzero action on H1). Thus F′ splits as ω_E plus a nonzero finite-support quotient, giving a nonzero finite-support subsheaf of the CM sheaf F, impossible. Therefore H1(F′)=0. If F/F′ were nonzero, the long exact sequence would surject H0(F) onto its nonzero finite-support global sections; this map is zero because every global section belongs to F′. Hence F′=F. The image of ω_E is sF, so at e the quotient by globally generated sections equals s times itself with s in the maximal ideal. Nakayama makes that quotient zero. Every closed point was arbitrary, proving generation, and faithful field base change descends it. The proof supplies the omitted finite-support and adjunction details in the source and does not assume E reduced or geometrically integral.''',source={'title':'The Stacks Project, Algebraic Curves: vanishing-twist, globally-generated and tensor-omega-with-globally-generated-invertible; complete omitted-detail argument','url':'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stacks/stacks-project/master/curves.tex'})
rows[curve]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[curve]['deps']+['cor-flat-local-depth-additivity']))
cartier=add('lem-regular-base-surface-cartier-curve-canonical-adjunction','Canonical adjunction for a Cartier fibre curve',['lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base','lem-finite-closed-immersion-derived-coinduction-adjunction','lem-finite-length-duality-over-a-regular-local-base',compat,'thm-serre-duality-for-coherent-sheaves-on-projective-cm-scheme'],r'''For a normal projective surface modification $X$ over a finite normal local domain $A$ of a regular two-dimensional local ring $R$, let $E$ be a Cartier closed fibre with residue field $\kappa$ and conormal $L=O_X(-E)|_E$. Then $\omega_E=\omega_X|_E\otimes L^{-1}$ is a projective-curve canonical module, up to the harmless one-dimensional residue-field normalization. At a regular point of $X$ the surface canonical module is invertible.''',r'''The ideal sequence 0→O_X(-E)→O_X→O_E→0 is a length-one locally free resolution. Applying internal Hom(-,ω_X[2]) gives i!D_X=(ω_X(E)|E)[1], because multiplication by the Cartier equation is injective on the CM torsion-free ω_X. Closed-immersion coinduction identifies its duality pairing over R with that inherited from X. On κ, the regular-parameter Koszul resolution and finite-length duality give RHom_R(κ,R[2]) a simple one-dimensional κ-module in degree zero. Choose one nonzero identification of that line with κ; finite-ring coinduction then identifies the inherited pairing with the usual curve pairing over κ. By its representing property this is the curve canonical module. A different line identification rescales it by a unit and changes none of the assertions. The conormal is L, so O_X(E)|E=L^-1 and the formula follows. For the last assertion work at a closed point of an embedding in projective space over R. Its ambient local ring T is regular and its quotient B=O_X,x is regular. Lift a minimal basis of the kernel of m_T/m_T²→m_B/m_B² to c=dim T−dim B elements of the defining ideal. They extend to regular parameters of T. The quotient by them is a regular local domain of dimension dim B and surjects onto B; its remaining prime kernel has height zero and hence is zero. Thus the defining ideal is that regular sequence. Its Koszul dual Ext is free of rank one over B, so the concentrated canonical module is invertible. Localization proves the assertion at every regular point.''')
surj=add('lem-rational-singular-point-blowup-canonical-pullback-surjective','Canonical pullback is surjective after blowing up a rational singular point',['lem-rational-normal-surface-point-blowup-normal-and-fibre-cohomology','lem-normal-surface-trace-cokernel-dualizes-h1-and-bounds-it',curve,cartier,compat,'thm-serre-vanishing','thm-nakayama-lemma'],r'''For a nonregular rational normal local surface domain in the permitted regular-base dualizing setting, let $f:X\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ be its ordinary point blowup, $E$ its exceptional divisor and $I=O_X(1)$. Then $H^1(X,\omega_X\otimes I^n)=0$ for $n\ge0$ and the canonical evaluation $f^*\omega_A\to\omega_X$ is surjective. The assertion localizes to closed rational singular points on a projective normal modification of the same regular base.''',r'''The rational blowup helper gives normal X and a CM projective curve E with H0(O_E)=κ, very ample L=I|E, and degree at least two because A is nonregular. Cartier adjunction gives ω_X|E=ω_E⊗L. For n≥0 the curve twist ω_E⊗L^(n+1) has zero H1: the globally generated positive-degree L^(n+1) is nontrivial (its degree is positive by tensor additivity). Tensor the Cartier ideal sequence to obtain 0→ω_X(n+1)→ω_X(n)→ω_E⊗L^(n+1)→0. The curve vanishing makes H1(ω_X(n+1))→H1(ω_X(n)) onto. Serre vanishing starts at large n and downward induction proves all stated vanishings. In particular H0(ω_X) surjects onto H0(E,ω_X|E). The degree-at-least-two curve helper makes the latter restriction globally generated. Hence the cokernel of global evaluation vanishes on E by Nakayama; its support is proper over the local base and misses the closed fibre, so is empty. Rational trace identifies H0(ω_X)=ω_A, proving the canonical evaluation surjective. For the localization assertion use the global rational trace compatibility helper: over a rational local point Rg*O=O, and its compatible trace identifies the localized pushforward of the global canonical module with the local canonical module. The Cartier-curve pairing and its one-dimensional residue normalization are unchanged; the same local proper argument therefore applies. No finite-over-R hypothesis is imposed falsely on a local ring of an intervening modification.''')
rows[surj]['deps']=sorted(set(rows[surj]['deps']+['cor-degree-additive-proper-curve']))
principal=add('lem-rank-one-torsion-free-surface-module-principalized-by-an-ideal-blowup','A rank-one surface module is principalized by an ideal blowup',['thm-pullback-center-ideal-invertible','def-blowup-scheme-along-ideal','thm-blowup-projective'],r'''For a finite torsion-free rank-one module $M$ over a Noetherian domain $A$, there is a nonzero ideal $J$ and a blowup $b:Y=\operatorname{Bl}_J\operatorname{Spec}A\to\operatorname{Spec}A$ such that $b^*M$ modulo torsion is invertible; the same holds on every integral model dominating $Y$.''',r'''Choose an identification M⊗Frac(A)=Frac(A). Torsion-freeness embeds M in that field. Clear denominators in a finite generating family to identify M, up to multiplication by one nonzero scalar, with a nonzero ideal J⊂A. The ordinary blowup universal construction makes JO_Y invertible. The map b*M→JO_Y is generically an isomorphism, so its kernel is torsion. On every integral affine chart any element of its kernel is killed by a nonzero scalar (clear its zero generic germ); conversely any torsion element maps to zero in the invertible torsion-free module. Thus the kernel is exactly torsion and the quotient is invertible. Pulling further to an integral dominating model gives the same argument with the invertible pullback ideal. This uses a fractional ideal and the blowup universal property, not a general Fitting or flattening theorem.''')
goren=add('lem-rational-normal-surface-reduced-to-invertible-canonical-module','Rational normal surfaces reduce to an invertible canonical module',['lem-rational-surface-local-rings-propagate-by-point-sequence-spreading','lem-rational-normal-surface-point-blowup-normal-and-fibre-cohomology','lem-normalized-point-blowups-dominate-local-normal-surface-modifications','lem-normal-projective-surface-dualizing-module-over-regular-local-base',compat,surj,principal,cartier],r'''A rational normal local surface domain in the permitted regular-base dualizing setting admits a finite sequence of ordinary point blowups at singular closed points, with each model normal and projective, whose terminal canonical module is invertible. Its closed local rings remain rational; an invertible canonical module with finite injective dimension makes them Gorenstein.''',r'''The canonical module ω_A is finite torsion-free generic rank one. Principalize it by the fractional-ideal blowup helper and dominate the finite normalization of that blowup by normalized point blowups. Rationality propagates to the local rings of every normal model, and the rational point-blowup normality helper shows every normalization step is the identity, so the sequence consists of ordinary blowups. At a singular terminal point x, each ancestor is singular: a point blowup of a regular surface stays regular, and an isomorphism does too. At each ancestor the map to the next model is either an isomorphism locally or the blowup of that rational singular point. The canonical surjectivity helper, together with composition of the rational canonical evaluations, therefore gives a surjection from the terminal pullback of ω_A onto ω_terminal at x. Its torsion-free rank-one quotient on the principalizing model is invertible; the dominating model has the same invertible quotient L. Since ω_terminal is torsion-free, the surjection factors through L_x. A surjection of this invertible module onto a torsion-free rank-one module is an isomorphism: trivialize L_x, whose surjection has generically zero kernel and hence zero kernel in the local domain. At regular terminal points the canonical module is invertible by the regular-point part of Cartier adjunction. Thus it is invertible everywhere. To retain only singular centres, delete any regular-centre step and all later centres over it: its entire descendant region is regular by regular point-blowup preservation. The retained centres lie in the unchanged open complement, where their point blowups agree with the original sequence; glue them to the identity on the deleted region. This is precisely the global point blowup at each retained closed centre, since its ideal is the unit ideal on that region. Canonical invertibility still holds on the unchanged singular region and on the regular deleted region. The finite final sequence therefore has only singular centres. Rationality of its closed local rings follows from propagation. Finally an invertible canonical module identifies, after local trivialization, the local dualizing complex with the local ring shifted by two; its finite injective dimension means the local ring has finite injective dimension, the Gorenstein condition. No rational-double-point resolution or quadratic-chart termination is asserted by this reduction.''')
